<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SpotBugs project decisions: a rule, or a rule in part of the code, that the team has decided not
     to enforce. One Match per decision, with a comment giving the reason, for example:

       <!- - JPA entities share associations by design (reason ...) - ->
       <Match><Package name="~team\.projectpulse\..*"/><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/></Match>

     Unlike the baseline, this file may grow, but every entry is a reviewed decision. -->
<FindBugsFilter>
  <!-- D1: EI_EXPOSE_REP and EI_EXPOSE_REP2 are off for the whole project. The rule asks for defensive copies of
       mutable fields, and on 2026-11-01 its 219 findings fell into four groups, none of which wants one:
       - 144 on JPA entities. Their getters return associations and collections that Hibernate manages; a copy
         would make changes such as getSections().add(x) invisible to dirty checking and orphanRemoval.
       - 27 on Spring constructor injection. Storing the shared singleton bean that was passed in is the point.
       - 36 on records (DTOs, ReminderService.ReminderResult) that Jackson builds, serializes and discards within
         one request.
       - 12 on plain classes. PeerEvaluationAverage and WeeklyPeerEvaluationReport are built per request, and
         MyUserPrincipal wraps the user entity. DocumentTemplate was the one real case: the singleton
         DocumentTemplateRegistry handed every caller the same mutable template. It is now an immutable record.
       The project has no java.util.Date, Calendar, Timestamp or array fields, the cases this rule exists for.
       SpotBugs filters cannot match an annotation such as @Entity, so the exclusion cannot be narrower than this.
       Revisit if the project adds a cache or another long-lived object handed out to callers. -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
